Yes. The useful abstraction is not that every modality is literally text, but that they can often be routed through a shared intermediate representation.

Think of an LLM as a kind of semantic compiler/orchestrator:

video
→ events + objects + motion + timing + camera + sound + intent
→ shared semantic representation
→ text / storyboard / scene graph / edit instructions / generation prompt
→ video again

The same thing can run the other direction:

text
→ characters + environment + temporal sequence + spatial relations + camera behavior + emotional trajectory
→ rendering system
→ frames over time

So your core insight is right: once the underlying structure has been made explicit, you can make one modality speak for another.

The important correction is that modern video models usually are not merely “text tokens projected onto a screen.” They may internally use visual tokens, latent patches, diffusion or transformer representations, temporal embeddings, audio tokens, and other machinery. But at the orchestration layer, an LLM can still operate as the common control language.

That gives us a clean architecture:

Multimodal Intermediate Representation

raw modality
→ extract invariant structure
→ canonical semantic form
→ reinterpret / transform
→ decode into chosen modality

For a video, that canonical form might contain:

who
what
where
when
motion
causal sequence
camera
sound
affect
style
uncertainty

Then you can do interesting things like:

video → prose
video → screenplay
video → animation instructions
video → another visual style
video → audio description
video → symbolic narrative
video → action plan

And because the representation is editable, you can modify the latent structure before rendering:

original scene
→ change pacing
→ change point of view
→ change emotional tone
→ preserve causal structure
→ rerender

That is essentially the same Lift–Play–Return operator we’ve been building:

surface artifact
→ abstract representation
→ manipulate
→ reinstantiate

The interesting part is that the LLM becomes less “a text chatbot” and more the interface language for heterogeneous cognition and generation systems.

Text is just one particularly convenient control surface.